  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nLow clouds are hanging on this morning across the coastal basin with \nareas of high clouds streaming to the northeast across the area. \nCloud coverage could be fairly persistent today for some areas as\na closed upper level low move east across the US West Coast. \nThere were a few sprinkles across the coastal basin this morning\nwith continued precipitation to our east in the desert Southwest,\nbut there is less than 15 percent chance of accumulating \nprecipitation through the afternoon. Under the influence of the \nlow, the marine layer will stay 3000 to 3500 ft deep into Tuesday \nmorning with low clouds expected to fill in much of the coastal \nbasin overnight. \n\nHighs today will be a few to around 5 degrees cooler than Sunday and \nas much as 5 to 10 degrees below average for the valleys. High \ntemperatures on Tuesday will warm a few to around 5 degrees with the \ngreatest warming for the inland valleys. Warming will continue on \nWednesday with high temperatures as much as another 5 to 10 degrees \nwarmer for the Inland Empire and inland Orange County. With the \nwarming, high temperatures on Wednesday will be within a few degrees \nof average for most areas. Another few degrees of warming is \nexpected Thursday and Friday with temperatures along the coast with \nthe highest departure from normal at 7 to 12 degrees. For the latter \nhalf of the week, overnight lows will increase to the mid-60s to \n70 west of the mountains and in the upper 70s to low 80s in the \nlow desert. \n\nFor Thursday, a closed low lingering to our southwest will start to \nmove east. This low will have some upper level moisture entrained in \nit which may bring some precipitation to the area, most likely for \nthe mountains. The low will bring some increase in instability, so a \nslight chance of isolated thunderstorms (15-20%) has been introduced \nto the forecast, mainly confined to the mountains. There is low \nconfidence in any potential impacts, especially in the way of \nflooding. The spread in NBM guidance between 10th percentile and \n90th percentile for 24 hour rainfall is 0 and 0.55 inches \nrespectively. \n30\nTropical Cyclone Polo, off the southwest coast of Mexico, is \nforecast by the National Hurricane center to become a major \nhurricane over the new few days with a track eventually and \ngenerally toward the west northwest which might put the center a few \nhundred miles south of the southern tip of Baja early Saturday. \nThere remains a lot of uncertainty in the forecast for late in the \nweekend into next week but there could eventually be at least some \nperipheral impacts to portions of Southern California.",
